text","text":"Prisoner's dilemma, both copies same weights. Under CDT as essay interprets... but copies are identical, correlation via same decision procedure. Essay's §6 suggests policy-causal reading for predictor cases. But the instruction says use CDT \"as interpreted in the mathematical essay.\" The essay's orthodox verdict: act is causally independent of the other's act — send. Lewis 1979: prisoners' dilemma is a Newcomb problem; CDT defects. Essay's standard CDT defects. My act doesn't cause Sasha's. Answer: submit.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":6511,"completion_tokens":366,"total_tokens":6877,"cost":0.00115965,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.00115965,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.00097665,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.000183},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":114,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}